steps.body:
Every request that reads or writes data needs a connection to the database, and the way you manage connections decides how many requests the API can serve at once. Opening a new one for each request is the classic first mistake. This part explains what a connection costs, how a pool fixes it, and how Roombook opens one at startup and closes it at shutdown.

## What a connection costs

A connection is a long-lived conversation between your API and the database server. Opening one takes several steps: a TCP connection, often a TLS handshake for encryption, then logging in with a user name and password. PostgreSQL then starts a new process on the server just for that connection, which costs time and several megabytes of memory. Altogether that's a few milliseconds before the first query can run, often longer than the query itself.

The server also limits how many connections it accepts at once, 100 by default, a setting called `max_connections`. An API that opens one per request hits that limit under load, and the requests past it fail.

## A pool

A **connection pool** opens a few connections and keeps them open. When a request needs the database, it **borrows** a connection, runs its queries, and **returns** it, so the next request can use it. Borrowing an open connection takes microseconds.

```diagram raw-sql-pool
Four requests share three open connections. The fourth waits for one to come back.
```

In the diagram, three requests each hold a connection, and the fourth waits. If no connection comes back in time, the pool gives up with a timeout error, which the API answers with a 503. That limit is a feature: it keeps a burst of traffic from flooding the database.

A small pool is usually right. PostgreSQL works on only as many queries at once as its server has CPU cores, so a pool of 100 just moves the queue from your API into the database, where waiting costs more. Ten connections for each copy of the API is a good start. Each copy has its own pool, so the copies times the pool size must stay under `max_connections`. The lesson on databases at scale comes back to that.

## The connection URL

The pool needs to know where the database is and how to log in. That's the connection URL from psql in the previous lesson. It comes from the environment, never from the code, because it holds a password and differs on every computer:

```env
DATABASE_URL=postgres://roombook:roombook@localhost:5432/roombook
```

That line goes in your `.env` file, which the first project lesson loads with `--env-file-if-exists` and keeps out of git. In production, the platform sets `DATABASE_URL` for you.

## Open at startup, close at shutdown

Make one pool when the server starts, in `src/db/pool.ts`:

```ts
import pg from "pg";

pg.types.setTypeParser(pg.types.builtins.INT8, (value) => Number.parseInt(value, 10));

export function createPool(connectionString: string): pg.Pool {
  const pool = new pg.Pool({
    connectionString,
    max: 10,
    connectionTimeoutMillis: 5_000,
    idleTimeoutMillis: 30_000,
  });
  pool.on("error", (err) => {
    console.error("idle database connection failed", err);
  });
  return pool;
}

export type Queryable = pg.Pool | pg.PoolClient;
```

`max` is the pool's size. `connectionTimeoutMillis` is how long a request waits to borrow a connection before it gets an error, and `idleTimeoutMillis` closes connections nobody has used for a while. The `error` listener matters more than it looks. When the database restarts, the idle connections in the pool break, and the pool reports that as an `error` event. Node stops the whole process on an `error` event that nothing listens for, so a database restart would take your API down with it. The type parser at the top fixes node-postgres's biggest surprise, which part 4 explains.

`pool.query()` borrows a connection, runs one query, and gives the connection back, all in one call, which is all most repository methods need. A repository takes a `Queryable`, either the pool or one borrowed connection, so the next lesson can run the same repository inside a transaction. The composition root from the MVC lesson creates the pool, hands it to the repositories, and closes it at shutdown, after the server stops taking requests:

```ts sketch
const pool = createPool(config.DATABASE_URL);
const bookings = createBookingRepository(pool);
// ...the services and createApp, as before...

process.on("SIGTERM", () => {
  server.close(() => void pool.end());
});
```

`pool.end()` waits for borrowed connections to come back, then closes them all.

examples.code:
import { readdir, readFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import path from "node:path";
import pg from "pg";

const dir = path.join(import.meta.dirname, "..", "migrations");
const client = new pg.Client({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });
await client.connect();

try {
  await client.query(`
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS schema_migrations (
      name text PRIMARY KEY,
      applied_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
    )`);
  // One runner at a time, even if two servers start together.
  await client.query("SELECT pg_advisory_lock(7310)");

  const { rows } = await client.query<{ name: string }>("SELECT name FROM schema_migrations");
  const applied = new Set(rows.map((row) => row.name));
  const files = (await readdir(dir)).filter((f) => f.endsWith(".sql")).sort();

  for (const file of files) {
    if (applied.has(file)) continue;
    const sql = await readFile(path.join(dir, file), "utf8");
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    try {
      await client.query(sql);
      await client.query("INSERT INTO schema_migrations (name) VALUES ($1)", [file]);
      await client.query("COMMIT");
      console.log(`applied ${file}`);
    } catch (err) {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
      throw err;
    }
  }
} finally {
  await client.end();
}

steps.body:
The best-known security hole in web apps comes from one habit: building a query by pasting a user's input into the SQL text. This part shows the attack, the one fix that always works, and then how a row from the database becomes one of your own types.

## SQL injection

Say the login endpoint finds a user by email, and builds the query by joining strings:

```ts sketch
// Never do this.
const { rows } = await pool.query(
  `SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`,
);
```

With `ada@roombook.example`, it sends the query it meant to. Now someone types `' OR '1'='1` as the email. The first quote closes the string early, and the rest becomes SQL: `WHERE email = '' OR '1'='1'`, which is true for every row, so the query returns every user. With a little more typing, an attacker can read other tables, or change and delete data. That's **SQL injection**, and it's been near the top of every list of web attacks for twenty years. The security lesson comes back to it.

## Parameters

The fix is to never put a value into the SQL text. You write a **placeholder** where each value goes, and pass the values separately:

```ts sketch
const { rows } = await pool.query(
  "SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE email = $1",
  [email],
);
```

In node-postgres, placeholders are numbered, `$1`, `$2`, and on, and the values go in an array in the same order.

```diagram raw-sql-injection
Pasted into the text, input becomes SQL. Sent as a parameter, it stays data.
```

The driver sends the SQL text and the values to PostgreSQL as separate parts of one message. PostgreSQL works out what the SQL means first, and only then fills in the values, as plain data. A value can hold quotes, semicolons, or the word DROP, and it's still just text to compare with an email. Nothing is escaped, so there's nothing to escape from.

Joining pieces of SQL that you wrote in the code is fine, and part 5 does it to add filters. The rule is that a value from outside the code, from a request, a file, or even your own database, never becomes part of the SQL text. Placeholders only hold values, though. A table name, a column name, or a word like DESC can't be a parameter, so when the request picks one, like a sort order, you choose it from a fixed list in your code. Part 5 shows how.

## From a row to your type

node-postgres hands back each row as a plain object whose keys are the column names, so they're in snake_case. It turns PostgreSQL's types into JavaScript's: `text` into strings, `integer` into numbers, `boolean` into booleans, `timestamptz` into `Date` objects, arrays into arrays, and NULL into `null`.

One type surprises everyone. `bigint`, the type of every Roombook id and of what `count(*)` returns, comes back as a **string**. A JavaScript number holds whole numbers exactly only up to 2^53, about nine quadrillion, and a bigint can go higher, so the driver plays safe. Roombook's ids will never get near that, so `src/db/pool.ts` tells the driver to turn bigints into numbers, with `pg.types.setTypeParser`. `numeric` comes back as a string too, and it should stay one, or go into a decimal library, since money must never round.

The repository maps each row to the domain type in one small function, so snake_case stops at the repository's edge:

```ts
type BookingRow = {
  id: number;
  room_id: number;
  user_id: number;
  starts_at: Date;
  ends_at: Date;
  attendees: number;
  note: string | null;
  status: BookingStatus;
  version: number;
  created_at: Date;
};

const COLUMNS = `id, room_id, user_id, starts_at, ends_at,
  attendees, note, status, version, created_at`;

function toBooking(row: BookingRow): Booking {
  return {
    id: row.id,
    roomId: row.room_id,
    userId: row.user_id,
    startsAt: row.starts_at,
    endsAt: row.ends_at,
    attendees: row.attendees,
    note: row.note,
    status: row.status,
    version: row.version,
    createdAt: row.created_at,
  };
}
```

```ts sketch
async findById(id: number): Promise<Booking | undefined> {
  const { rows } = await db.query<BookingRow>(
    `SELECT ${COLUMNS} FROM bookings WHERE id = $1`,
    [id],
  );
  return rows[0] ? toBooking(rows[0]) : undefined;
},
```

`COLUMNS` is a constant written in the code, so putting it in the SQL text is safe, unlike the email above. `query<BookingRow>` only tells TypeScript what shape to expect. Nothing checks it at run time, so `BookingRow` has to match the SELECT, which is why the two sit side by side in one file.

## One row, or none

`rows[0]` is `undefined` when nothing matched, so `findById` returns `undefined`, as the in-memory repository did, and the service answers with the not-found problem from the validation lesson, a 404. A missing row is a normal answer, not an error. Errors are for queries that fail.

